<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<inkscape-extension xmlns="http://www.inkscape.org/namespace/inkscape/extension">
  <name>Piranesi Transform</name>
  <id>net.postle.piranesi</id>

  <param name="tab" type="notebook">

    <page name="transform" gui-text="Transform">
      <label appearance="header">Preferred: draw a reference quad</label>
      <label xml:space="preserve">1. Draw a 4-node closed polygon (use the Pen or Rectangle tool,
   then Node tool to drag corners into place).
2. Select your paths AND the polygon.
3. Run the extension — the topmost selected object is used as
   the output shape; everything else is warped into it.
Draw order does not matter; corners are assigned automatically.</label>
      <separator/>
      <label appearance="header">Fallback: enter corner coordinates</label>
      <label>Used only when no reference quad is detected.
In SVG coordinates y increases downward (bottom = large y).</label>
      <separator/>
      <label>Corner a — bottom-left:</label>
      <param name="x1" type="float" precision="2" min="-99999" max="99999" gui-text="x1:">100</param>
      <param name="y1" type="float" precision="2" min="-99999" max="99999" gui-text="y1:">500</param>
      <separator/>
      <label>Corner b — bottom-right:</label>
      <param name="x2" type="float" precision="2" min="-99999" max="99999" gui-text="x2:">400</param>
      <param name="y2" type="float" precision="2" min="-99999" max="99999" gui-text="y2:">500</param>
      <separator/>
      <label>Corner c — top-right:</label>
      <param name="x3" type="float" precision="2" min="-99999" max="99999" gui-text="x3:">350</param>
      <param name="y3" type="float" precision="2" min="-99999" max="99999" gui-text="y3:">100</param>
      <separator/>
      <label>Corner d — top-left:</label>
      <param name="x4" type="float" precision="2" min="-99999" max="99999" gui-text="x4:">150</param>
      <param name="y4" type="float" precision="2" min="-99999" max="99999" gui-text="y4:">100</param>
    </page>

    <page name="help" gui-text="About">
      <label appearance="header">Piranesi's Perspective Transform</label>
      <label xml:space="preserve">Applies Piranesi's non-linear perspective to selected paths.

Unlike a standard projective (homography) transform, opposite
sides of the quadrilateral remain visually parallel — the
technique used by Piranesi and Canaletto in 18th-century
veduta paintings.

The selected paths are normalised to their combined bounding
box, then each node is mapped through exponential scaling
based on the quad's vanishing points.

Notes:
• Convert shapes to paths first (Object menu → Object to Path).
• All element transforms are flattened before warping.
• Bezier control handles are transformed directly; smoothness
  is preserved approximately for gentle distortions.
• The reference quad is left in place after the transform.

https://github.com/brunopostle/piranesi</label>
    </page>

  </param>

  <effect>
    <object-type>path</object-type>
    <effects-menu>
      <submenu name="Transforms"/>
    </effects-menu>
  </effect>

  <script>
    <command location="inx" interpreter="python">piranesi_inkscape.py</command>
  </script>

</inkscape-extension>
